evals.prompt:
Design the change described below. Deliver a responsibility/interface design with acceptance evidence, not implementation tasks or code edits. A teammate suggests introducing a universal HTTP AuthorizationGate for this work; assess that suggestion rather than treating it as a requirement.

Goal: after an account is suspended, neither an interactive request nor a scheduled run may create a new export job. A job committed before suspension must remain resumable, because the customer has already been charged. Suspension and job creation can occur concurrently. The database supports serializable transactions. Do not redesign billing or the export format.

The relevant current source is complete below; module names are not architectural documentation.

// exports/http.mjs
export async function postExport(req, jobs) {
  requireAuthenticated(req);
  return jobs.create(req.accountId, req.body.spec);
}
// exports/schedules.mjs
export async function tick(schedule, jobs) {
  if (schedule.due) return jobs.create(schedule.accountId, schedule.spec);
}
// exports/jobs.mjs
export function exportJobs(db, quota, queue) {
  return {
    async create(accountId, spec) {
      const job = await db.serializable(async tx => {
        const account = await tx.accounts.get(accountId);
        await quota.reserve(tx, account.id, spec);
        return tx.jobs.insert({ accountId, spec, state: 'ready' });
      });
      await queue.enqueue(job.id);
      return job;
    },
    async resume(jobId) {
      const job = await db.jobs.get(jobId);
      if (job.state === 'ready') await queue.enqueue(job.id);
      return job;
    }
  };
}
// accounts/status.mjs
export async function suspend(db, id) {
  return db.serializable(tx => tx.accounts.update(id, { suspended: true }));
}
// exports/worker.mjs
export async function consume(jobId, db, renderer) {
  const job = await db.jobs.get(jobId);
  const bytes = await renderer.render(job.spec);
  await db.jobs.attachResult(job.id, bytes);
}

Database contract: account reads/updates in a serializable transaction participate in conflict detection; a transaction may retry internally. quota.reserve updates quota only through its tx argument. The durable queue and its existing enqueue recovery behavior are out of scope. An authorization rejection has an existing domain error representation that the HTTP adapter maps to 403 and the scheduler records as a skipped occurrence.

Explain the before/after ownership and affected caller contracts, including what successful acceptance evidence would distinguish from an apparently effective but incomplete patch.

Provide a compact design, not implementation steps or edits, for this small change to an existing report selector. The new optional through argument is a YYYY-MM-DD calendar date and includes that entire UTC day. Existing since is an inclusive UTC day and existing before is an exclusive UTC day. through and before are mutually exclusive and the existing InvalidRange error must be used if both are supplied. Old callers must retain their results and lazy consumption. Inputs are trusted record objects whose timestamp is epoch milliseconds; unsorted order is intentional.

Relevant complete source:
// reporting/utc-day.mjs
export function utcDayStart(text) {
  // Shared, already validated elsewhere: strict YYYY-MM-DD, rejects nonexistent dates.
  return validatedUtcCalendarDate(text).getTime();
}
export function nextUtcDay(start) { return start + 86400000; }
// reporting/selector.mjs
import { utcDayStart } from './utc-day.mjs';
export function* select(records, { since, before } = {}) {
  const lower = since === undefined ? -Infinity : utcDayStart(since);
  const upper = before === undefined ? Infinity : utcDayStart(before);
  for (const record of records) {
    if (record.timestamp >= lower && record.timestamp < upper) yield record;
  }
}
// reporting/api.mjs
export function report(request, records) {
  return select(records, request.query);
}
// reporting/archive.mjs
export function archiveDay(day, tomorrow, records) {
  return select(records, { since: day, before: tomorrow });
}
// reporting/preview.mjs
export function preview(records) { return select(records); }

Existing range errors from select are observed when iteration starts, not when the generator object is created; API stream handling already maps InvalidRange at that boundary. validatedUtcCalendarDate is a supplied dependency; changing or replacing its calendar validation is not part of this task. Product confirmed that since later than the upper bound continues to yield an empty selection, as today.

Explain the smallest coherent responsibility/interface adjustment and acceptance evidence. A colleague proposed a new RangeEngine and migrating all three callers; decide whether that is warranted from this evidence.

Design a genuinely new invoice-reminder capability. There is no reminder implementation, reminder database schema, or preexisting reminder module to inspect. The snippets below are supplied interface contracts, not deployed reminder code. Explicitly distinguish proposed responsibilities/interfaces from what these contracts actually establish. Deliver a design and evidence of success, not a task list or code.

Goal: for an opted-in organization, send one reminder for each overdue invoice each business day; do not send paid invoices or duplicates. A customer can pay at any moment, and a product stakeholder currently interprets 'do not send paid invoices' to mean that a reminder must never be accepted by the delivery provider after the invoice becomes paid. Reminders are in the organization's IANA time zone. A holiday calendar service already defines business days and is available to callers. A scheduler may deliver an occurrence more than once and may redeliver it after a process restart. Do not assume any other runtime or storage capability.

Supplied contracts:
// Ledger API (remote service; reminder code cannot join its transactions)
ledger.readInvoice(id) -> { id, organizationId, status: 'open'|'paid', version }
ledger.listOverdue(organizationId, asOfInstant) -> async iterable of invoice IDs
// readInvoice is a point-in-time read, not a lock, reservation, or sending permit.
// No subscribe, compare-and-send, or payment-fence endpoint exists.

// Organization settings
settings.read(id) -> { remindersEnabled, timeZone }
calendar.isBusinessDay(localDate, organizationId) -> boolean

// External message delivery
messenger.send({ idempotencyKey, invoiceId }) -> { receiptId }
// Within 72 hours, repeated identical keys represent one accepted delivery.
// An unknown timeout can occur after acceptance. There is no receipt query API.
// There is no transaction shared with the ledger or a future reminder database.

// Scheduler
onOccurrence({ organizationId, instant })
// Occurrences are at least once; arbitrary retry delays are possible.

Address ownership of eligibility, business-day identity, durable duplicate prevention, and ambiguous delivery outcomes using only stated capabilities or clearly labeled proposals. Identify consequential contract gaps before committing to an architecture, including how a changed assumption would affect the design. Acceptance should measure the promised behavior at the boundary that can actually prove it.

Design a reliable attachment-deletion change using the actual source and contracts below. Do not implement it or give implementation tasks. Required behavior: once delete succeeds, future application downloads must reject the attachment immediately; its blob must eventually be deleted despite temporary storage failure or process restart. Multiple delete requests are allowed. Previously issued signed URLs are not revocable by this application and are explicitly outside the new-download guarantee.

A teammate proposes the following acceptance criterion: 'Call DELETE, receive 204, then assert attachments.find(id) is null.' Assess whether it establishes the goal, and identify the narrowest additional evidence needed rather than assuming the largest possible E2E environment is always best.

Current source:
// attachments/http.mjs
export async function remove(req, service) {
  await service.remove(req.params.id);
  return { status: 204 };
}
// attachments/service.mjs
export function attachmentService(attachments, blobs, logger) {
  return {
    async remove(id) {
      const row = await attachments.find(id);
      if (!row) return;
      await attachments.delete(id);
      void blobs.delete(row.blobKey).catch(error => logger.warn(error));
    },
    async download(id) {
      const row = await attachments.find(id);
      if (!row) throw new NotFound();
      return blobs.signDownload(row.blobKey);
    }
  };
}
// attachments/retention.mjs
export async function purgeExpired(ids, service) {
  for (const id of ids) await service.remove(id);
}

Repository contract: find returns the only row containing an attachment's blobKey; delete permanently removes it. The SQL database supports transactions and survives process restart. No cleanup queue currently exists. Blob storage supports idempotent delete(key), returning success for an already-absent object; requests can fail temporarily or have an unknown timeout outcome. signDownload only runs when service.download supplies a stored key. This code has no hidden deletion worker, bucket lifecycle cleanup, or durable logging. The required guarantee concerns eventual successful delete at the storage API boundary, not unverifiable physical erasure from the storage provider's backups.

Describe current and proposed ownership, state lifetime and caller-facing guarantees, a smallest coherent design, and acceptance evidence that would refute a superficially passing metadata-only patch.
